Steering Diffusion Models to Rare Events with Sequential Monte Carlo Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.08652v1 [stat.ML] 06 Oct 2026 Steering Diffusion Models to Rare Events with Sequential Monte Carlo Aavash Subedi † † thanks: Correspondence to subedi@stats.ox.ac.uk . Affiliation: Department of Statistics Affiliation: University of Oxford Tim Reichelt Affiliation: Department of Physics Affiliation: University of Oxford Christopher Williams Affiliation: Department of Statistics Affiliation: University of Oxford Philip Stier Affiliation: Department of Physics Affiliation: University of Oxford Yee Whye Teh Affiliation: Department of Statistics Affiliation: University of Oxford Saifuddin Syed Affiliation: Department of Statistics Affiliation: University of British Columbia Abstract Diffusion models are increasingly used as surrogates for expensive simulators in weather prediction, molecular dynamics, and materials design. In these models, computing the probability p 0 ​ [ E ] p_{0}[E] of an event E E is difficult, especially when the event of interest is rare . A stable estimate using Monte Carlo becomes computationally intractable, requiring a growing sample size ∝ 1 / p 0 ​ [ E ] \propto\!1/p_{0}[E] to compensate for an increasing rarity. In this paper, we present D iffusion I mportance Sampling of R are E vents or DireSMC , a sequential Monte Carlo scheme that guides a population of weighted samples towards the rare event, giving access not only to samples but also to a calibrated estimate of its probability. We set up our guidance using an analytical relaxation of the event set, allowing the method to easily extend to a wide range of user-defined rare events. We validate our method on a toy problem with analytical solutions and on a score-based climate emulator, where we obtain accurate rare-event probabilities on a range of rarities from 10 − 3 10^{-3} to 10 − 5 10^{-5} , achieving net speed-ups of 9 × 9	imes to 1413 × 1413	imes over Monte Carlo. 1 Introduction Score-based diffusion models ( Song et al., 2021b ) are a powerful tool for generative modelling, and have become standard in the quantitative sciences as cheap surrogates for expensive simulators in numerical weather prediction ( Couairon et al., 2026 , Price et al., 2024 ) , molecular simulation ( Abramson et al., 2024 ) and materials design ( Zeni et al., 2025 ) . Trained on data from a distribution p 0 p_{0} , they generate new representative samples by running a denoising process p t p_{t} that anneals from a Gaussian reference p 1 p_{1} down to the data p 0 p_{0} , driven by a learned score ∇ x ​ log ​ p t 
abla_{x}\log p_{t} . Often, scientists are interested in events E E lying in regions where the data distribution assigns little mass p 0 ​ [ E ] p_{0}[E] . Such rare events carry outsized downstream impacts, and arise across a wide range of domains, from the environmental sciences ( Manshausen et al., 2026 , Lancelin et al., 2026 , Peard et al., 2026 ) and bioinformatics ( Xie et al., 2026 ) to finance ( Glasserman and Li, 2005 ) , engineering ( Au and Wang, 2014 ) and autonomous driving ( O'Kelly et al., 2018 ) . Practitioners typically need two things: representative samples of the event, to characterise possible failure modes, and a calibrated estimate of its probability p 0 ​ [ E ] p_{0}[E] , to inform planning and resource allocation ( Kaplan and Garrick, 1981 , Rubino et al., 2009 ) . The simplest approach is to draw N N independent samples and form a Monte Carlo (MC) average to estimate p 0 ​ [ E ] p_{0}[E] , which is difficult precisely because the event is rare. The relative error is 1 / N ​ p 0 ​ [ E ] 1/\sqrt{Np_{0}[E]} , hence stabilising the variance requires N ∝ 1 / p 0 ​ [ E ] N\!\propto\!1/p_{0}[E] samples, which is prohibitively expensive ( Morio et al., 2014 , Rubino et al., 2009 ) . This has motivated a large body of research designed specifically to tackle the poor scaling of standard MC ( Kahn and Harris, 1951 , Morio et al., 2014 , Glasserman and Li, 2005 ) . Diffusion model steering offers a natural method for generating samples from E E without the high costs of simple MC. A reward r 0 r_{0} defines a tilted target π 0 ∝ p 0 ​ e r 0 \pi_{0}\propto p_{0}\,e^{r_{0}} , and inference-time guidance steers a pre-trained sampler towards it. At each denoising step, the gradient of a time-dependent reward r t r_{t} is added, so that the sampler targets π t ∝ p t ​ e r t \pi_{t}\propto p_{t}\,e^{r_{t}} ( Uehara et al., 2025 , Chung et al., 2023 , Song et al., 2023 ) . However, designing a good reward is difficult as the ideal r t r_{t} is often intractable, and any approximations used can guide the sampler towards a different distribution to π 0 \pi_{0} . For rare-event sampling specifically, setting the tilting term e r 0 ≡ 𝟏 E e^{r_{0}}\equiv\mathbf{1}_{E} recovers the rare-event tilted distribution p 0 ( ⋅ | E ) ∝ p 0 𝟏 E p_{0}(\cdot|E)\propto p_{0}\mathbf{1}_{E} that we wish to sample from. However, this reward is a poor object for steering, as its gradient cannot be used to steer particles towards E E . Figure 1: We steer particles ( ∙  ullet ) from p ref p_{\mathrm{ref}} towards π 0 \pi_{0} , which concentrates mass in the rare-event region of interest, using a guidance drift ∇ x r k {\color[rgb]{1,0,0}
abla_{x}r_{k}} . Particle size reflects the importance weights accumulated along the trajectory, which are used to resample ( × 	imes ) and form a rare event estimate, p 0 ​ [ E ] p_{0}[E] . Recent works have attempted to address these problems. The error of using an imperfect reward r t r_{t} can be addressed using sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) by tracking importance weights along the denoising trajectory and resampling particles to focus on high probability regions ( Wu et al., 2023 , He et al., 2026 , Singhal et al., 2025 , Skreta et al., 2025 ) . In diffusion-based molecular simulation, Xie et al. (2026) adapt ideas from Abrams and Bussi (2013) , Shirts and Chodera (2008) to estimate the occupancy of a rare metastable state using collective variables and a sequence of events E i E_{i} converging to E E . Manshausen et al. (2026) instead steer a diffusion model using the probability-flow ODE (PF-ODE) ( Song et al., 2021a ) , and estimate p 0 ​ [ E ] p_{0}[E] using importance sampling. These methods have high compute costs, which become especially problematic in the rare event settings of interest here. Contributions. First , we present DireSMC where we use a sequence of tilted marginals π t \pi_{t} to construct an unbiased sequential Monte Carlo estimator of p 0 ​ [ E ] p_{0}[E] that runs within the denoising process, obtaining both samples and probability estimates. We show that the variance is governed by the quality of our steering rather than the rarity. Second , we show how to design a reward for rare-event simulation, and in particular for threshold-exceedance events E = { x : ϕ ⁡ ( x ) ≥ τ } E\!=\!\{x:\phi(x)\geq	au\} , giving a smooth surrogate whose look-ahead guidance is closed-form under a Tweedie posterior approximation ( Song et al., 2023 , Chung et al., 2023 ) . Third , we empirically validate on: (1) a Gaussian mixture where ground truth is available and we obtain probability estimates that do not degrade across 10 − 3 10^{-3} – 10 − 5 10^{-5} orders of rarity. (2) a score-based climate emulator, where we draw samples and estimate probabilities of compound hot-and-dry extremes over the region of the 2021 Pacific Northwest heatwave, with net speed-ups of 9 × 9	imes to 1413 × 1413	imes over Monte Carlo across rarities from 10 − 3 10^{-3} to 10 − 5 10^{-5} . 2 Background Diffusion models. Let p data p_{\mathrm{data}} be the true data generating distribution. A diffusion model ( Song et al., 2021b ) progressively noises the data along a path p t p_{t} interpolating from p data p_{\mathrm{data}} at t = 0 t=0 to a Gaussian reference p ref p_{\mathrm{ref}} at t = 1 t=1 . Sampling reverses this process: a X 1 ∼ p ref X_{1}\sim p_{\mathrm{ref}} draw is evolved to t = 0 t=0 , under the reverse-time SDE, d ​ X t = [ μ t ​ ( X t ) − σ t 2 ​ ∇ x ​ log ⁡ p t ​ ( X t ) ] ​ d ​ t + σ t ​ d ​ W ↼ t , t ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] , dX_{t}= ig[\mu_{t}(X_{t})-\sigma_{t}^{2}\,
